## 7) Ops notes / best practices

- Stable naming: keep namespace consistent.
- Column lineage: enable in dbt, Spark where possible.
- Backfill: run crawler first, then let pipelines add run history.
- Idempotency: stable Job/Dataset IDs → clean merges.
- Security: scrub PII, restrict Marquez API/UI.

What’s different, then?

1) API layer & data model focus
	•	Marquez (OpenLineage)
	•	API focus: OpenLineage events & lineage graph.
	•	Model: Jobs, Runs, Datasets, DatasetVersions, Lineage edges, Facets (JSONB).
	•	Typical flow: Producers emit OL events → Marquez API persists → Marquez UI queries API to render lineage.
	•	Open Data Discovery (ODD)
	•	API focus: Broader catalog (datasets, jobs, dashboards/BI, owners, tags), plus lineage.
	•	Model: Rich catalog entities (data_entity, data_source, ownership, tags, lineage tables, custom metadata).
	•	Typical flow: Collectors push catalog metadata → ODD Backend API persists → ODD UI queries API for catalog + lineage.

2) Service topology
	•	Marquez
	•	Usually 2 services: API (backend) and Web/UI.
	•	UI calls the Marquez API (same deployment).
	•	ODD
	•	Usually 3 services: ODD Backend, ODD UI, Postgres.
	•	UI calls the ODD Backend; collectors also call the backend.

3) Endpoints & contracts
	•	Marquez UI → Marquez API
	•	Endpoints expose OpenLineage-first resources (namespaces, jobs, runs, datasets, events).
	•	ODD UI → ODD Backend
	•	Endpoints expose catalog resources (entities, ownership, tags, BI objects) and lineage views.

4) Metadata ingestion path
	•	Marquez
	•	Ingestion = OpenLineage events (push at runtime from Spark/Airflow/dbt, etc.).
	•	ODD
	•	Ingestion = Collectors (poll/pull from systems) or custom pushes to ODD’s ingestion API.

5) What the DB stores (both PostgreSQL, but schema differs)
	•	Marquez Postgres: lineage-centric (jobs/runs/datasets + JSONB facets).
	•	ODD Postgres: catalog-centric (entities, sources, ownership, tags, dashboards) plus lineage relationships.

Airflow: runs DAGs and (with openlineage-airflow) emits lineage events for tasks.
	•	PySpark: reads/writes the lake and (with the Spark agent/listener) emits lineage events for inputs/outputs.
	•	Data Lake: just holds your files/tables—no calls to OpenLineage.
	•	Marquez (OpenLineage API): receives events, normalizes them, persists to Postgres.
	•	Postgres: stores metadata only (jobs, runs, datasets, lineage edges, facets).
	•	Marquez UI: queries the Marquez API to render lineage graphs & details.

🧩 What Each Piece Does — Open Data Discovery (ODD)
	•	Airflow:
Runs your DAGs and orchestrates jobs (including PySpark tasks).
You can use the ODD Airflow Collector (or a custom metadata script) to extract job metadata — DAG IDs, task names, owners, schedules — and send it to the ODD Backend API.
(Unlike OpenLineage, it doesn’t emit “events”; it sends static metadata snapshots.)

⸻

	•	PySpark:
Executes transformations — reads/writes data from the Data Lake (e.g., S3, ADLS, or GCS).
ODD doesn’t instrument PySpark directly; instead, metadata about PySpark jobs or their output datasets can be collected by:
	•	The ODD Spark Collector (if you have Spark’s metastore/catalog available), or
	•	A custom script that registers those datasets and transformations in ODD.
The goal is to describe what data assets exist and how they relate, not track live runs.

⸻

	•	Data Lake (S3/GCS/ADLS/Delta/Iceberg):
Stores your actual data files and tables.
It never calls ODD — instead, ODD Collectors connect to it (via APIs or metadata catalogs like Hive/Glue) to pull metadata such as:
	•	Dataset names and paths
	•	Columns and schemas
	•	Owners, tags, creation timestamps
	•	Upstream/downstream lineage relationships

⸻

	•	ODD Collectors:
These are small services or scripts that connect to your data sources (Airflow, Spark, dbt, Snowflake, Tableau, etc.), extract metadata, and send JSON payloads to the ODD Backend API.
Example collectors:
	•	Airflow Collector
	•	dbt Collector
	•	BigQuery / Snowflake Collectors
	•	Custom Python collector for your Spark or S3 jobs

⸻

	•	ODD Backend (API Server):
The core engine of ODD.
	•	Receives metadata from collectors via REST API.
	•	Normalizes entities (datasets, jobs, dashboards).
	•	Stores lineage and relationships.
	•	Writes everything to the PostgreSQL metadata DB.
	•	Exposes endpoints for the UI to query.

⸻

	•	PostgreSQL (Metadata Database):
Stores only metadata, not data.
Examples of stored entities:
	•	Datasets (names, columns, schema, owners)
	•	Data sources (S3 buckets, databases, etc.)
	•	Jobs (Airflow DAGs, Spark pipelines)
	•	Lineage links (input → output)
	•	Tags, descriptions, ownership, dashboards
It serves as the single source of truth for all metadata the ODD platform manages.

⸻

	•	ODD UI:
The frontend web application that connects to the ODD Backend API (not directly to Postgres).
It lets you:
	•	Search and browse all datasets, jobs, and dashboards
	•	Visualize data lineage across Airflow, PySpark, and your data lake
	•	See ownership, tags, and documentation
	•	Filter by source systems or domains